## Interfaces in C# – The “Contract” of a type

| Feature | What it is | Why it matters |
|---------|------------|----------------|
| **Definition** | A *contract* that specifies *what* a type must expose (methods, properties, events, indexers) but not *how* it does it. | Lets you program to an abstraction rather than a concrete implementation. |
| **Syntax** | `interface IFoo { void Bar(); int Baz { get; set; } }` | Clean, explicit, and can be shared across assemblies. |
| **Implementing** | A class or struct writes `: IFoo` and then provides bodies for all members. | You get a guarantee that the class will meet the contract. |
| **Inheritance** | Interfaces can inherit from other interfaces. | Build up a rich, reusable contract hierarchy. |
| **No state** | Interfaces cannot contain fields (though they can have default interface methods starting with C# 8). | Keeps the contract purely about behavior. |
| **Multiple inheritance** | A type can implement many interfaces. | Combine unrelated capabilities in one type. |
| **Polymorphism** | Code that uses the interface type doesn’t care about the concrete implementation. | Enables loose coupling, easier testing, DI, etc. |

---

## A *simple* example

Imagine we want a handful of geometric shapes that can all be *drawn* on a screen.  
We don’t care whether a shape is a circle, a rectangle, or something else – we only care that it can be drawn.

```csharp
// 1. Declare the contract
public interface IShape
{
    // All shapes must be able to draw themselves.
    void Draw();

    // Optional: let the caller ask for the shape's area.
    double Area { get; }
}
```

### Implement the interface

```csharp
// Circle implements IShape
public class Circle : IShape
{
    public double Radius { get; set; }

    public Circle(double radius) => Radius = radius;

    public double Area => Math.PI * Radius * Radius;

    public void Draw()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Drawing a circle with radius {Radius}");
    }
}

// Rectangle implements IShape
public class Rectangle : IShape
{
    public double Width  { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }

    public Rectangle(double width, double height)
    {
        Width  = width;
        Height = height;
    }

    public double Area => Width * Height;

    public void Draw()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Drawing a rectangle {Width}×{Height}");
    }
}
```

### Use the interface

```csharp
public static void Main()
{
    List<IShape> shapes = new List<IShape>
    {
        new Circle(3),
        new Rectangle(4, 5)
    };

    foreach (var shape in shapes)
    {
        shape.Draw();          // Polymorphic call – the right method runs
        Console.WriteLine($"Area = {shape.Area}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Drawing a circle with radius 3
Area = 28.274333882308138
Drawing a rectangle 4×5
Area = 20
```

> **Key takeaway** – the code that loops over `shapes` only knows about `IShape`.  
> It doesn’t need to know whether the concrete type is a `Circle`, `Rectangle`, or anything else that might come later.

---

## Why use an interface instead of an abstract class?

| Aspect | Interface | Abstract class |
|--------|-----------|----------------|
| **Multiple inheritance** | ✔ a class can implement many interfaces | ❌ only one base class |
| **State** | ❌ cannot hold fields | ✔ can hold fields (and thus maintain state) |
| **Default members** | ✔ since C# 8 you can give a body | ✔ (normal in abstract classes) |
| **Use case** | “A thing can do X” (behavior) | “A thing is a kind of Y” (inheritance chain) |

In the example above, shapes *are* shapes; there is no sensible hierarchy that requires an abstract base class (except maybe a `Shape` base class that holds common fields like a name).  
Using an interface keeps the code flexible and allows any type – even a struct – to participate.

---

## Advanced – explicit interface implementation (optional)

Sometimes you want to expose a member only when the type is seen through the interface:

```csharp
public class Square : IShape
{
    public double SideLength { get; set; }

    public double Area => SideLength * SideLength;

    void IShape.Draw()          // explicit
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Drawing a square {SideLength}×{SideLength}");
    }
}
```

Now `Square` can also expose its own `Draw` method:

```csharp
public void Draw()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Draw called directly on Square");
}
```

Only when you cast to `IShape` will the interface version be called.

---

## Bottom line

- **Define** an interface when you want to specify *what* a type must be able to do, without dictating *how* it does it.
- **Implement** it in any class or struct; the compiler guarantees the contract.
- **Use** the interface type in code that consumes those objects – you get polymorphism, loose coupling, and easier testability.

Happy coding! 🚀
